<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wangen im Allgäu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wangen im <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> is a town in the Allgäu region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> which grew from a settlement under the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a> and became a free imperial city in the 13th century. It remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1802 and to Württemberg in 1810, and keeps a well-preserved old town with painted gate towers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wangen-im-allgau.jpg" alt="Wangen im Allgäu" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Lorer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wangen_i_a_air16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wangen im <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> lies in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, in the Westallgäu, on the north bank of the Obere Argen. The Untere Argen flows past the north-west of the city and unites with the Obere Argen to its south-west. The city is second in size in the Ravensburg district and a centre for the surrounding communities. Its name was officially given as Wangen in Allgäu in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place was first mentioned in 815 as Wangun in a monastery document. In 1217 a royal document declared that Wangen should remain in royal hands, and in 1286 King Rudolph I granted it the status of a free imperial city. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its growth was helped by its position at the crossroads between Ravensburg, Lindau, Leutkirch and Isny and by the growing trade through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Scythes and canvas were its main manufactured exports, and they gave the city a large trade surplus, which it used to buy lands beyond its walls as a safeguard against economic swings.</p><p>During the German Mediatisation of 1802 Wangen lost its free status and was incorporated into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>; in 1810 it changed hands to the Kingdom of Württemberg. From 1938 to 1972 it was the seat of the Wangen rural district, and in 1973 the state government designated it a Große Kreisstadt, a large district town, because its population had passed 20,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Despite major fires in 1539, 1793 and 1858, the old town still joins architecture from the Early Middle Ages to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. The Oberstadtkirche St. Martin, one of the oldest buildings, was already present in the 9th century and contains Romanesque and Gothic work. The Ravensburg Gate, the city's main landmark, was first mentioned in 1472 and probably took its present appearance in 1608, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> decoration; the Lindau Gate and the Pfaffenturm tower are of similar age. A local history museum, opened in 1974 in a former mill, shows the original mill mechanisms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wangen im Allgäu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wangen</b> grew around a settlement linked to the Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall, which held rights there from the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wangen">Free Imperial City of Wangen</a></li></ul></section>
